The reviewer's approach is right. To avoid a new grant policy, reuse the existing OS hand-over path:

1. **preload.cjs:** require `webUtils` and expose `openDroppedFile(file)`. It computes `const p = webUtils.getPathForFile(file)` inside the preload. If `p` is empty, it returns null. Otherwise it returns `ipcRenderer.invoke('desktop:open-dropped', p)`. The page must never be able to send a raw path: the channel is only reachable through this function, and webUtils returns '' for any File built in JS.

2. **main.cjs:** add `handle('desktop:open-dropped', async (_e, p) => {...})`. It should:
   - run `assertPath(p)` (isSafeAbsPath, so no UNC paths);
   - run `fs.stat`, then `if (!st.isFile()) throw`, so no FIFOs, devices or folders;
   - check the extension is one the app opens (pgfx, psd, psb, png, jpg, jpeg, webp, gif, bmp);
   - `return readOpened(p)`. That reuses the existing rule: write access only for .pgfx (by extension or magic bytes), read access for images and PSDs so Open Recent can reopen them.
   - optionally call `app.addRecentDocument` for .pgfx, as deliverOpen does.

3. **DropOverlay.tsx:75:** in the desktop app, before reading the file, compute the path. If `samePath`/focusOpenProject matches an already-open session, switch to it and skip the read and parse. Otherwise call `openFileWithIo({ path: opened?.path ?? null, name: f.name, data }, ...)`. Use the bytes returned by main, or keep `f.arrayBuffer()` and only take the path. Fall back to `path: null` when there is no desktop bridge or no path (browser build).

Opening with a real path then gives saving back to the dropped file, Open Recent and focusOpenProject deduplication for free. Also add a drop case to scripts/electron-desktop-check.mjs using CDP Input.dispatchDragEvent with `data.files`; it works under xvfb (see drop-path.mjs).

In src/render/engine.ts, make docItems use the same inline/isolated decision as compositeList (engine.ts:2141-2154), which counts only *shown* clipped layers. Then any flip of that decision changes the raw item list, and planIncremental returns null, forcing a full composite:

```ts
/** Whether shown layers are clipped to ids[i] (compositeList then composites it as an isolated clip base). */
function hasShownClipped(rc: RC, ids: ID[], i: number): boolean {
  for (let j = i + 1; j < ids.length; j++) {
    if (ids[j] === rc.below) return false;
    const c = rc.doc.layers[ids[j]];
    if (!c || !c.clipped) return false;
    if (isShown(rc, c)) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
// docItems:
if (l.type === 'group' && isShown(rc, l) && inlinePassThrough(l) && !l.clipped && !hasShownClipped(rc, ids, i)) { ...inline markers... }
```

Groups that are clipped, or that have shown clipped layers, become one normal item (sig = listSig, layer = l, region = groupExtent or 'full'). That over-approximation is safe, and those groups were already composited isolated, so performance is about the same.

Add a regression test on a pass-through group with a Multiply child that extends past an opaque sibling, plus a variant with an adjustment child. Compare renderDocument against a render after invalidateRenderCache() after each of these steps:
- (a) clipping a small layer above the group
- (b) releasing it
- (c) toggling that clipped layer's visibility
- (d) toggling `clipped` on the group itself
- undo/redo of each

The reviewer's main fix, wrapped in try/finally. In drawContent's group case (src/render/engine.ts:1663-1667):

```ts
const stopped = rc.stopped;
rc.stopped = false;
try {
  compositeList(rc, l.childIds, acc);
} finally {
  rc.stopped = stopped;
}
```

The caller's compositeList already sets rc.stopped after a group that contains `below` (engine.ts:2162), so stopping at `below` still works at every level. The alternative of resetting the flag at the start of recomposite() would only cover that one caller. Add a regression test: render a Drop Shadow group holding a raster layer and an adjustment as `below` with renderDocument({below}), make a patch edit on the raster, render again, and compare with a render after invalidateRenderCache(). Run it with background on and off and at 0.5x scale (see rv-render-paint-diff-v3/below.js).
